    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_3a3def5fb4b128b5c82893d85923908a43ae780ab6ade5e444dfe386a460f2ad",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The matrix-counting interface records ensemble symmetry as admissibility constraints. Unitary truncation counts arbitrary nonnegative k by k matrices with independent row and column budget inequalities, producing a degree-k^2 polynomial G_k; symplectic moments instead count symmetric nonnegative matrices with even diagonal, and symplectic truncation produces a degree-k(k+1)/2 polynomial F_k. Thus the change of symmetry class reduces the parameter space and adds parity structure rather than merely relabeling the same count.",
